# Hypomnema

Keep these constraints unless the user changes them.

- The client is a Flutter app.
- Import UI widgets from `package:material_ui/material_ui.dart`.
- The app connects to Firebase. Persistent data lives in Cloud Firestore.
- Phone and desktop are both required form factors. Build a layout for each; a phone screen stretched to desktop width is not enough.

## Responsive UI: balanced abstraction

Keep the same abstraction level on both form factors and one layer of decision in one place.

- Name shell builders `_phoneLayout` and `_desktopLayout`, matching `login_screen.dart`. When one form factor gets an extracted shell or pane helper, give the other a symmetric helper (`_phoneListPane` / `_desktopListPane`, not only `_listPane` on desktop).
- Order private helpers top-down: shells (`_phoneLayout`, `_desktopLayout`), then dispatch (`_entriesBody` or equivalent), then pane/content builders. Keep `Scaffold` and breakpoint choice in `build` unless both layouts need the same wrapper.
- Put cross-screen layout numbers in `lib/core/constants/` (for example `layout.dart`). Application code uses that constant and small helpers (`desktopLayoutForWidth`, `desktopLayoutFor`); do not scatter literal breakpoints. `lib/core/constants/` holds product constants only—not test widths, fixtures, or other test-only values.
- In tests, import the same constant and derive viewport widths from it (`kDesktopLayoutMinWidth`, `kDesktopLayoutMinWidth + 1`, `kDesktopLayoutMinWidth - 200`, and so on). When a loop exercises multiple widths, every value should be an expression on that constant, not a lone magic number beside it.
- Do not leave one branch fully extracted and the other inlined in `build`. Do not grow a single method with unrelated phone and desktop trees; split shells and panes instead.

## Project layout

- Organize `lib/` feature-first. Keep generated `lib/firebase_options.dart` at the package root; FlutterFire writes that path.
- Mirror that layout under `test/`. A test for `lib/features/auth/ui/login_screen.dart` lives at `test/features/auth/ui/login_screen_test.dart`.
- When the same or very similar test data is used in more than one test file, put one shared fake or fixture in `test/helpers/` and reuse it. Do not copy it into each file.

```text
lib/
├── main.dart                  # App entry point and initialization
├── firebase_options.dart      # Generated. Do not move.
├── app/                       # Root widget, shell, global providers
│   ├── app.dart
│   └── shell.dart             # Signed-in shell and its scaffolds
├── core/                      # App-wide essentials and shared utilities
│   ├── constants/
│   ├── errors/
│   ├── network/               # API clients, interceptors
│   ├── theme/
│   └── utils/
├── shared/                    # UI and models reused across features
│   ├── models/
│   └── ui/
└── features/                  # One folder per business feature
    └── auth/
        ├── data/              # Repositories, data sources, DTOs
        ├── domain/            # Entities and business logic
        └── ui/                # Screens, feature widgets, view models
```

- Put a new feature under `lib/features/<feature>/{data,domain,ui}/`. Add a layer directory when it has code.
- `core/` is shared infrastructure. `shared/` is shared UI and models. Feature code does not go in either.
- Place a login screen at `lib/features/auth/ui/login_screen.dart`. Place a repository at `lib/features/auth/data/`.
- A feature navigates inside itself. The login screen switching between sign-in and password reset stays in `features/auth/ui`.
- Cross-feature navigation belongs in `app/`: the auth gate, the signed-in shell, and a future route table. A feature reaches another feature's screen through that composition, and `app/` may depend on the other feature's screen or repository while assembling the tree.
- The commit gate is `make web`, then `make check`. Write that into each root `PLAN.md`. Every commit-gate command must succeed with no warning or error, except one warning that must also be written into that gate: the Firebase CLI notice that another emulator suite is already running for project `kws-hypomnema`. The rules tests start their own emulator on port 8180 while the dev suite stays on port 8080. That notice does not fail the gate.
